I-III-IV-VI

I’d shift back to including the phonetic/accent/footnote options. The shared plot-critical element is best read as a word-boundary oronym: Asimov’s “The Next Day” turns on “tomorrow” / “Tom Morrow,” and Christie’s “The Thumb Mark of St. Peter” turns on “pile of carp” / “pilocarpine,” with the haddock/St. Peter clue leading to the fish phrase. That is fundamentally a phonetic mishearing/wordplay problem, not merely a title-to-surname semantic clue.

So the practices that can in principle handle it are:

- **I**: transcreation can create an analogous target-language oronym/pun;
- **III**: changing/localizing the setting can let an analogous target-culture wordplay function naturally;
- **IV**: making a character foreign or noticeably accented can motivate a comparable mishearing;
- **VI**: phonetic-transcription footnotes can explain the original sound relation to target-language readers.

**II** preserves English audio but does not by itself make the clue intelligible in the translated text, and **V** cannot convey a phonetic/verbal pun. Thus the capable set is **I-III-IV-VI**.

